WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.830
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.582
I'm going to try something
a little different.

00:00:08.582 --> 00:00:12.440
I'm going to tell you a story, but
it's a different kind of story.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.370
It's very short,
it only has three sentences.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.769
Are you ready?

00:00:16.769 --> 00:00:18.322
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:00:18.322 --> 00:00:18.822
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Jake.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.193
He went to the store.

00:00:23.193 --> 00:00:25.255
Fred died.

00:00:25.255 --> 00:00:30.340
Sharon went hungry and wept.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.330
Now, in listening to this story,

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.650
what can you all tell me about maybe
the relationship between Fred and Sharon?

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:38.430
Any ideas?

00:00:39.515 --> 00:00:40.736
Does anybody think they were married?

00:00:40.736 --> 00:00:43.155
Ted Ming, yeah.

00:00:43.155 --> 00:00:46.110
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; So

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.089
did you think that Fred was the one
who went to the grocery store?

00:00:52.238 --> 00:00:54.906
And you also thought that the store
that Fred was going to was a grocery

00:00:54.906 --> 00:00:55.554
store, right?

00:00:55.554 --> 00:00:59.410
So in looking at this story,

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.380
I didn't actually say any of that.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.210
In these three simple sentences,

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:11.240
Ted interpreted some things
that weren't necessarily there.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.620
Now, our brains are actually
hardwired to do this.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.490
Now imagine that you're trying to convince
someone of your side of an argument,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.100
or trying to explain something,
some concept to somebody.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.400
People may be interpreting things
that aren’t necessarily there.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.100
So what’s going on here?

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.292
What can science tell us about how we’re
interpreting this information, and

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:36.287
how can we use that information
to tell better stories?

00:01:40.704 --> 00:01:46.010
&gt;&gt; Our brains are pre-disposed to think
in story terms and in story structures.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.423
This impacts how we understand things,
how we make sense of things, and

00:01:49.423 --> 00:01:54.470
what we choose to remember and
what we choose to forget.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.020
These story terms and structures,
they provide a sense of order

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.550
to information that's coming to
us that's otherwise disorderly.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.290
Now Kendall Haven and his team of
researchers, who are the leading

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.860
researchers on this topic,
they've done an extensive amount of work.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.290
And they've found that this story-like
structure that our brains are so

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.120
accustomed to, they're the result
of over 100,000 years of evolution.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.400
They've also found the specific
part of our brain that's in charge

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.790
of applying this story-like structure,
it's called the Neural Story Net.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.230
And the neural story net has
one very important goal.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:36.030
It needs to satisfy
the Make Sense Mandate of our brains.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.950
So what is the Make Sense Mandate?

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.670
It's very simple, actually.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.950
It just says that everything we see and

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.960
hear needs to make sense before we
actually understand and learn it.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.320
It's pretty simple, right?

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.379
Things just need to make
sense before we learn it.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.045
But what's more interesting to
observe is how our neural story net

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:04.311
impacts the information that we're
receiving if it's disorderly.

00:03:04.311 --> 00:03:07.846
How does it manipulate information that's
told to us in order to force it to

00:03:07.846 --> 00:03:08.472
make sense?

00:03:11.063 --> 00:03:13.230
Let's take Dillon's example.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.940
He gave you three sentences,
completely disjointed and disorganized.

00:03:18.056 --> 00:03:21.520
Your neural story nets started making
certain assumptions about those

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.650
three sentences in order to
make it make sense to you.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.300
He went to the store.

00:03:28.054 --> 00:03:29.910
Fred died.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.306
Sharon went hungry and wept.

00:03:32.306 --> 00:03:35.881
You made the assumption that Fred and
Sharon were married.

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:38.630
You made the assumption that
the story was a grocery store.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.090
Dillon didn't actually say any of that,
but you assumed it.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.780
That's your neural story net in action.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.540
That's it working to try to bring
some connection between these

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.570
disjointed sentences.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.340
Now it can get worse than this.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:00.100
If the story being told to you is so
disjointed, if it's so disorganized,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:04.370
your brain may have to work so hard that
it just decides, hey, let's disengage.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.790
Let's forget about what's
being told to us right now.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.170
It might tell your body to
slouch back in your chair.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.373
It might tell your hand to reach into
your pocket, pull out your iPhone,

00:04:15.373 --> 00:04:18.380
you just play around on it and
forget about it, forget about class.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.740
Now look, that's all well and good if
we're the listeners in the audience.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.570
If we're the listeners, sure, we can just
store some information, we can make some

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:31.180
assumptions, can maybe just forget about
things and play back on our iPhones.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.110
But what if we're the communicators?

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.650
What if we're the ones who

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.420
need to send some very important
message to the audience?

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.818
What if we have a crucial message and
we can't let the audience disengage?

00:04:42.818 --> 00:04:45.720
We can't let them distort our information.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.830
If that's the case,
then we need some sort of strategy.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.810
We need to come up with something
to be able to fit our message into

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.480
a story-like structure.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:57.750
Because it's your job, as storytellers,
to connect all the dots for your audience.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:05.100
So Kendall Haven, in all his research,
he's come up with four major concepts

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:09.190
which govern how well a message is
delivered and whether that delivery

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.370
fits into the story-like structure
that our brains are accustomed to.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:14.321
So let's learn about those
four major concepts.

00:05:18.262 --> 00:05:24.123
&gt;&gt; The four concepts are rooted in one
belief, and that is the listener's ability

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:29.740
to make sense of our story, directly
impact our ability to influence them.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.420
So the first step, engagement.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.293
Now engagement does not necessarily
guarantee influence automatically, but

00:05:39.293 --> 00:05:41.700
it is certainly the first
step to making it possible.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.680
So what is engagement?

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.886
Engagement is our ability
to physically show up.

00:05:48.886 --> 00:05:53.319
It's our intentive focus on making sure
that when we're connecting with our

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:56.742
listeners and
we're physically being engaged with them.

00:05:56.742 --> 00:05:59.801
No matter how complex and
nuanced the story is,

00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:04.782
to no matter how simple the story is,
like Dylan's three-sentence story.

00:06:04.782 --> 00:06:09.286
What we've learned in class is that there
are a couple of different ways in which we

00:06:09.286 --> 00:06:11.324
can actually, physically engage.

00:06:11.324 --> 00:06:14.800
Through the Use of Pauses,

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.939
our Tone of Voice, our Hand Gesturing,

00:06:19.939 --> 00:06:24.495
our Eye Contact, and our Posture.

00:06:24.495 --> 00:06:29.820
Transportation, what

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.500
we learned from the neural story net is
that listeners are constantly trying to

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.650
figure out what information we need to
throw away and what information we need

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.750
to keep to actually make sense
of a story being told to us.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:45.010
What transportation enables
us to do as storytellers,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:49.880
what you as storytellers,
is it helps a listener visualize and

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:53.420
contextualize the story and
the plot line that they're being put into.

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:56.400
And that can happen in various formats.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.730
In class, we learn through visual
word plays as one example.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:03.260
To even Mark Bezos physically,
overtly, bringing us and

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:05.920
transporting us into his storyline
as a firefighter in his TED talk.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:12.600
Ultimately, transportation
is aimed at building trust

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.440
between the story, the storyteller and
the narrative being told.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.810
So with engagement, with transportation,

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:25.250
Ash will speak a bit more about how to
deliver content that is influencing.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:31.540
&gt;&gt; So
the third point in this is relevance.

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:32.852
It is self-explanatory.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.870
As Judy says there's aim,
understand your audience,

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.910
design your intent and
narrate your message accordingly.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.430
So your content has to be
relevant to your audience

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.660
because our mind has a make sense mandate.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:53.550
Our mind is constantly asking this
question, what's in it for me?

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.350
How am I related to this story?

00:07:56.380 --> 00:08:02.430
If there is no connection, the mind
can outrightly reject your message,

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:06.940
and do something as Jeff said, play with
the mobile phone or completely disconnect.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:11.277
But if you can make sense
with your audience,

00:08:11.277 --> 00:08:16.727
if you can stay relevant to your audience,
you can completely sell your idea.

00:08:16.727 --> 00:08:18.200
The fourth is influence.

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.200
This is the whole purpose of your message.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.440
This is what you're trying
to do with your story.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.637
You want to influence their behavior.

00:08:25.637 --> 00:08:29.770
You want to make your audience do certain
things in the way you want them to do.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.909
But is it the only behavior
that we want to address?

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:36.760
Probably not.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.721
Behavior is only a manifestation.

00:08:39.721 --> 00:08:43.099
We want to address their attitudes.

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:46.060
We want to address their beliefs,
their knowledge.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:48.140
We want to alter that.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.940
We want to create a parallel
stream of their beliefs.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.404
Okay, what you're saying,
what you're thinking is right, but

00:08:54.404 --> 00:08:56.200
let us think it this way.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.510
Do not try to correct that.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.120
Do not try to say, no, what you're saying
is wrong, what I'm saying is right.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:02.620
That doesn't sell, no.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:06.335
You have to alter it,
you have to define a new balance tree.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:14.180
In summary,
we talked about three things today.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.970
Our brain analyzes every
message in story form.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:25.610
If it cannot connect with a story,
our message does not pass through.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:27.211
Number one message.

00:09:27.211 --> 00:09:31.836
Number two,
based on if our idea makes sense,

00:09:31.836 --> 00:09:39.307
based on the make sense mandate in our
brain, our message can be accepted,

00:09:39.307 --> 00:09:44.303
altered or
completely rejected by our audience.

00:09:44.303 --> 00:09:49.005
And third, we can craft good
stories if we follow a structure.

00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:50.666
If we can stay engaged,

00:09:50.666 --> 00:09:55.650
if we can transport our audience to
the place where we want to take them,

00:09:55.650 --> 00:10:01.740
it stays relevant and influence their
attitude, belief and knowledge.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:07.193
We can craft excellent stories
that can influence our audience.

00:10:07.193 --> 00:10:08.392
Thank you.

00:10:08.392 --> 00:10:13.469
[MUSIC]

